          %h2#idea Idea
          %p The construction of Čech homology used coverings of the space by families of open sets. The way the open sets overlap gives ‘combinatorial’ information on the space.
          %p
            This has been abstracted and extended to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            and also in the direction of algebraic geometry. This article aims to introduce some of the main ways this idea has interacted with some of the many themes in the nLab.
          %h2#history_ech_1930s History (Čech 1930s)
          %p
            The term ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+nerve"}> nerve of a covering
            ’ has been used from the late 1920s for a construction that starts with a space and an open cover of it and builds a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complex"} simplicial complex
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complex"} simplicial complex
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduard+Cech"} Čech
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/P.+S.+Aleksandrov"} P. S. Aleksandrov
            so one often talks about Aleksandrov–Čech (co)homology. There was an alternative approach described by Vietoris (1927), which led to what is known as
            %span.newWikiWord
              Vietoris homology
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Vietoris+homology"} ?
            There are separate entries in nlab for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+homology"} Čech homology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+cohomology"} Čech cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+homology"} strong homology
            and in special cases it reduces to what was called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steenrod-Sitnikov+homology"} Steenrod-Sitnikov homology
            (but which was originally constructed in a different way).
          %h2#nerve_of_an_open_cover Nerve of an open cover
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Sergeevi%C4%8D+Aleksandrov"}> Pavel Sergeevič Aleksandrov
              , 1926) Given a (compact) space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and a (finite) open cover,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X,\alpha)
              %em nerve of the cover
              whose
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplices
              are
              = succeed "-strings" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n + 1)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle U_0, \ldots, U_n\rangle
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \in \alpha
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cap U_i \neq \emptyset
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
              is another cover such that for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\in \beta
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    U\in
                    \\alpha
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\subseteq U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\to U
              in this case defines a map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X,\alpha)\to C(X,\beta)
            %p
              dependent on the choice of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              for each
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %h2#application_of_ech_methods Application of Čech methods
          %p
            Applying one’s favourite homotopy functor,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathbf{S}\to \mathbf{A}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X,\alpha)
            and to these homotopy classes of induced transition maps, yields an inverse system of objects in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X,\alpha)
            by themselves do
            %em not
            define a pro-object in the category of simplicial sets. They do form a pro-object in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi sSets
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(sSets)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sSets
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+homotopy"} Čech homotopy
            for more discussion on this point.)
          %h3#ech_homology_and_cohomology Čech homology and cohomology
          %p Aleksandrov and Čech in the 1930s, applied homology and cohomology in this situation to extend simplicially-based homology to a much wider class of spaces. Lefschetz, without the language of category theory, studied, again in the 1930s, the formal properties of inverse systems of polyhedra and maps between them and his student Christie looked at the homotopy groups in this setting.
          %ul
            %li
              see
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+cohomology"} Čech cohomology
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+homotopy"} Čech homotopy
              for more on this.
          %h3#shape_theory_as_a_ech_homotopy_theory Shape theory as a Čech homotopy theory
          %p
            Borsuk (1968) developed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
          %p
            The first papers on this approach were by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vietoris+complex"} Vietoris complex
            of a space relative to an open cover as well as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+nerve"} Čech nerve
            itself. (By
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dowker%27s+Theorem"} Dowker's Theorem
            the two complexes give the same information up to homotopy, but the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vietoris+complex"} Vietoris complex
            is a functor on the category of covers, having values in the category of simplicial sets, whilst the Čech complex does not give so nice a structure as most naturally it takes values in the homotopy category of simplicial sets. In fact, that functor can be rigidified but this requires a certain amount of work.)
          %p
            Porter also gave a partial solution to the problem of the stability of a space, that is whether it has the same Čech homotopy as a polyhedron. The methods developed for that problem then suggested the existence of a homotopy theory for pro-simplicial sets related to homotopy coherence. That observation was also made by Edwards and Hastings, who proposed a strong shape theory based on what they termed
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Steenrod homotopy
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Steenrod+homotopy"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander-%C4%8Cech+duality"} Steenrod-Sitnikov homology
          %p
            (See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+homotopy"} Čech homotopy
            for references and more on this area).
          %h3#echlike_methods_in_algebraic_geometry Čech-like methods in algebraic geometry
          %p
            The use of modified Čech methods in algebraic geometry was well established when Grothendieck and his collaborators in Paris started adapting it to work with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            = succeed ".)" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Lubkin's construction
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Lubkin%27s+construction"} ?
          %p
            Given that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            is essentially about abstracting a notion of ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"}> covering
            ’, it is not surprising that modified Čech methods can be applied. Artin and Mazur used Verdier’s idea of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercover"} hypercovering
            to get, for each Grothendieck topos,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(\mathbf{S})
            (i.e. an inverse system of simplicial sets), which they call the étale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of the topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{E}
            (which for them is ‘sheaves for the étale topology on a variety’).
          %p
            Applying homotopy group functors gives pro-groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i(\mathbb{E})
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(\mathbb{E})
            is essentially the same as Grothendieck’s
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+fundamental+group"} algebraic fundamental group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔼
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(\mathbb{E})
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite group
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-object"} pro-object
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite group
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+groups"} localic groups
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} localic
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological
          %p
            Grothendieck’s nice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
            has thus an interpretation as a formal limit of a Čech type, or shape theoretic, system of
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
          %p
            Can
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
            (or its more powerfully structured ‘strong’ or ‘homotopy coherent’ version, cf. Lisica and Mardešić, Edwards and Hastings, Porter (papers 1976–78) be useful for studying étale homotopy type? Not without extra work, since the Artin–Mazur–Verdier approach leads
            %br>/
            one to look at inverse systems in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi proHo
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} proHo(\mathbf{S})
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+shape+theory"} strong shape theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hopro
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hopro(\mathbf{S})
            have been made (e.g. by Lubkin, (1967) or using simplicial schemes in Friedlander, (1982), but it is not completely clear if one of them is the definitive method.
          .query
            %p What is the consensus on this here? I have sometimes seen talks which claim to have THE method but none has been clearly THE ONE. Perhaps I did not go to the right conferences! (Some is needed here, e.g. for more ways of getting around the difficulty.)
          %p
            One of the difficulties with this hypercovering approach is that ‘hypercovering’ is a difficult concept and to the ‘non-expert’ seem non-geometric and lacking in intuition. Thankfully for us, there is an alternative approach put forward by Ken Brown (1973), (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} BrownAHT
          %p
            As the Grothendieck topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{E}
            ‘pretends to be’ the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sets
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Simp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Simp(\mathbb{E})
            as long as we take care of the arguments we use. To see a bit of this in action we can note that the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Simp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Simp(\mathbb{E})
            will be the constant simplicial sheaf with value the ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
          %p
            (a) constant sheaf, i.e. not varying ‘over
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{E}
            is thought of as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
          %p
            (b) constant simplicial object, i.e. each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_n
            is the same and all face and degeneracy maps are identities. Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
            interpreted as an étale space is the identity map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to X
            as a space over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Simp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Simp(\mathbb{E})
            can store a lot of information about the space (or site)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            One can look at the homotopy structure of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Simp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝔼
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Simp(\mathbb{E})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} BrownAHT
            showed it had a fibration category structure and if we look at those fibrant objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            in which the natural map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : K \to [0]
          %p
            is a weak equivalence, we find that these
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            are
            %em exactly
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercover"} hypercoverings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            give a simplicial set,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(K)
            and varying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            amongst the hypercoverings gives a pro-simplicial set (still in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi proHo
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} proHo(\mathbf{S})
            not in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hopro
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hopro(\mathbf{S})
            unfortunately) which determines the Artin–Mazur
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+homotopy+type"} pro-homotopy type
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{E}
          %h3#links_with_derived_category_theory Links with derived category theory
          %p
            This makes it clear there is a link between Čech methods and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            theory. In the first, the ‘space’ is resolved using ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"}> cover
            ings’ and these, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            theoretic setting, lead to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
            that are weakly equivalent to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of some functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \mathbf{C} \to \mathbf{A}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on objects weakly equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' : \mathbf{W}(C) \to \mathbf{A}
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
